WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.170 --> 00:00:04.840
- Last video, I was at the Ashton Graybiel
Spatial Orientation Lab at Brandeis University,

00:00:04.840 --> 00:00:07.620
and while I was there one
of the team just casually mentioned

00:00:07.620 --> 00:00:11.240
that they have an
artificial gravity laboratory.

00:00:11.250 --> 00:00:15.300
Artificial Gravity Facility sounds like
something out of science fiction,

00:00:15.300 --> 00:00:17.630
but it's not,
and there's very much a reason why

00:00:17.630 --> 00:00:20.420
I'm keeping my head straight
and forward at the moment.

00:00:20.420 --> 00:00:24.750
This is the Artificial Gravity Facility,
otherwise known as the rotating room.

00:00:24.750 --> 00:00:27.260
So, let's do some experiments.

00:00:27.260 --> 00:00:29.680
This is unscripted.
I don't know what's going to happen now,

00:00:29.680 --> 00:00:32.490
other than things are going very
weird when I move my arms.

00:00:32.490 --> 00:00:34.500
Okay, so it's a room that rotates.

00:00:34.500 --> 00:00:37.550
No one has invented science fiction
gravity generators yet,

00:00:37.550 --> 00:00:40.820
but if you have a theoretical
spaceship going to Mars

00:00:40.820 --> 00:00:43.050
and you wanted to
generate apparent gravity

00:00:43.050 --> 00:00:45.540
to keep astronauts healthy,
that's what you'd do.

00:00:45.540 --> 00:00:48.720
You would spin up the ship
and use centrifugal force.

00:00:48.720 --> 00:00:51.190
And that lab is the research prototype.

00:00:51.190 --> 00:00:56.140
How do humans deal with a
very strange gravitational environment?

00:00:56.140 --> 00:00:59.960
- The first thing that you should do is
try to push yourself off the wall

00:00:59.960 --> 00:01:03.420
and align your body with the
direction of the resultant.

00:01:03.420 --> 00:01:05.824
- Oh. Oh, okay!

00:01:05.824 --> 00:01:07.226
[laughter]

00:01:07.226 --> 00:01:08.800
- Now you're standing upright.

00:01:08.800 --> 00:01:11.640
- Wow. Can I walk, or is that gonna--

00:01:11.640 --> 00:01:13.493
- No, don't walk, it's a little dangerous.

00:01:14.350 --> 00:01:17.720
- It is almost impossible to envision

00:01:17.720 --> 00:01:22.010
long duration space flight
without artificial gravity.

00:01:22.010 --> 00:01:25.490
The best way to study
artificial gravity on Earth

00:01:25.490 --> 00:01:29.030
is to build a rotating environment.

00:01:29.030 --> 00:01:33.200
We started designing it in the mid-80s.

00:01:33.200 --> 00:01:37.430
Ideally we want to study its
effects on the human body

00:01:37.430 --> 00:01:41.870
and we want to learn also
how to pre-adapt astronauts

00:01:41.870 --> 00:01:43.960
to the force of artificial gravity.

00:01:43.960 --> 00:01:52.410
99.9% of those will be highly
dysfunctional individuals until adapted.

00:01:52.610 --> 00:01:55.120
- They have NASA-standard sick bags
for everyone, by the way.

00:01:55.120 --> 00:01:59.000
I was required to keep one in my
back pocket throughout just in case,

00:01:59.000 --> 00:02:01.380
because if you tilt your head too fast

00:02:01.390 --> 00:02:07.790
your inner ear has no idea what's going on
and things go... wrong, very quickly.

00:02:07.790 --> 00:02:11.267
- And now move your arm
and try to go as straight as you can.

00:02:11.267 --> 00:02:12.657
- [laughter]

00:02:12.657 --> 00:02:13.960
Oh, I moved my head.

00:02:13.960 --> 00:02:16.100
- You shouldn't move your head.
- Do you get used to this?

00:02:16.100 --> 00:02:18.650
- Yeah, yeah, you get used to it, yeah.
You can adapt.

00:02:18.650 --> 00:02:22.749
Move your arms and try to
feel this force that is...

00:02:22.749 --> 00:02:23.925
- Oh. That is...

00:02:23.925 --> 00:02:26.200
Okay, just to be clear,
I'm not putting this on,

00:02:26.200 --> 00:02:27.826
as far as I'm concerned,

00:02:27.826 --> 00:02:32.320
the signals I'm giving to my muscles are,
'move my arms forward'.

00:02:32.320 --> 00:02:34.790
And now I'm forcing them.

00:02:34.790 --> 00:02:37.570
But I'm pushing against
a force here, you know.

00:02:37.570 --> 00:02:42.470
- If you keep going, at some point
you won't feel the force anymore.

00:02:42.470 --> 00:02:44.030
- Oh that's weird.

00:02:44.030 --> 00:02:46.100
- That's weird, right?
- That's so weird.

00:02:46.100 --> 00:02:47.930
- So now we are adapted.

00:02:47.930 --> 00:02:51.060
- So if I try to do anything else,
I haven't adjusted to it.

00:02:51.060 --> 00:02:54.110
But that specific movement,
my body's got used to.

00:02:54.110 --> 00:02:57.450
- That's right.
- Wow.

00:02:57.450 --> 00:03:00.120
- One way to visualise this force

00:03:00.120 --> 00:03:03.300
is for me to try and throw a
little tennis ball at you.

00:03:03.300 --> 00:03:06.173
I will throw it straight at you,

00:03:06.173 --> 00:03:08.315
- Okay.
- and see what happens, okay?

00:03:08.315 --> 00:03:10.390
- Whoa!

00:03:10.390 --> 00:03:11.421
What?

00:03:11.421 --> 00:03:14.330
[laughter]

00:03:14.330 --> 00:03:16.747
I'm struggling to work this out. Wh...

00:03:16.747 --> 00:03:19.203
Because, in my head, this
is a normal reference frame.

00:03:20.079 --> 00:03:22.030
But it's clearly not.
- We are rotating.

00:03:22.030 --> 00:03:23.810
And now I'm gonna throw it over there.

00:03:23.810 --> 00:03:25.130
- Yeah.
- And hopefully

00:03:25.130 --> 00:03:26.539
it's gonna get at you.
- Okay.

00:03:26.539 --> 00:03:27.372
- Are you ready?

00:03:27.372 --> 00:03:29.619
- Yeah, throw it.

00:03:29.619 --> 00:03:30.840
- Yes!

00:03:30.840 --> 00:03:31.841
- Ah nice.
- Great.

00:03:31.841 --> 00:03:34.160
[laughter]

00:03:34.160 --> 00:03:35.410
- Wow.

00:03:35.410 --> 00:03:38.010
All those artefacts are
from the Coriolis force.

00:03:38.010 --> 00:03:41.040
It's not a real force acting
on the ball, but it looks like one

00:03:41.040 --> 00:03:43.710
when you're in that
weird rotating environment.

00:03:43.710 --> 00:03:47.340
Have a look at this 360° image of the lab.

00:03:47.340 --> 00:03:50.360
Now, it's a little distorted because it's from
a single camera in the middle,

00:03:50.360 --> 00:03:53.400
but it's close enough.
Here's the tricky part:

00:03:53.400 --> 00:03:57.560
the circumference of a circle is longer
the further out you go from the middle.

00:03:57.560 --> 00:04:00.940
But because everything in that circle
is rotating at the same speed,

00:04:00.940 --> 00:04:02.850
once around every six seconds,

00:04:02.850 --> 00:04:05.710
things on the outside have
farther to travel, so they're

00:04:05.710 --> 00:04:08.560
moving at a faster speed
than things on the inside.

00:04:08.560 --> 00:04:11.460
On the outside, we're moving pretty fast,
but that camera in the middle

00:04:11.460 --> 00:04:12.950
is just spinning on the spot.

00:04:12.950 --> 00:04:15.420
So let's mark the sideways
speed that everything

00:04:15.420 --> 00:04:17.360
is moving at, relative
to the rest of the world.

00:04:17.360 --> 00:04:19.550
Green is fast, red is slow.

00:04:19.550 --> 00:04:22.450
When you throw a ball across
that room it keeps that

00:04:22.450 --> 00:04:25.170
sideways speed that it
had when it left your hand

00:04:25.170 --> 00:04:27.990
as it travels into slower
moving areas of the room.

00:04:27.990 --> 00:04:29.050
Now, out at the edge, that was fine.

00:04:29.050 --> 00:04:31.040
It was going the same speed
as everything around it,

00:04:31.040 --> 00:04:33.450
so it looked like it aimed for the centre.

00:04:33.450 --> 00:04:36.730
But by the time it starts to
get there, it's a missile,

00:04:36.730 --> 00:04:40.010
flying outwards compared
to everything around it.

00:04:40.010 --> 00:04:41.570
Now from the outside that make sense.

00:04:41.570 --> 00:04:43.150
After it leaves your hand, the ball

00:04:43.150 --> 00:04:44.450
just moves in a straight line.

00:04:44.450 --> 00:04:48.460
From the inside it looks like there's a force
suddenly sending it sideways,

00:04:48.460 --> 00:04:50.660
and that is the Coriolis force.

00:04:50.660 --> 00:04:53.730
And it's not something your brain
has evolved to deal with.

00:04:53.730 --> 00:04:57.033
- Now, if you move your arms,
you're pretty much fine.

00:04:57.040 --> 00:04:59.080
- Oh! I...

00:04:59.080 --> 00:05:01.340
[laughter]

00:05:01.346 --> 00:05:04.196
- Just by moving around,
you have been adapting.

00:05:04.196 --> 00:05:08.360
- That's ridiculous.
Thank you so so much.

00:05:08.360 --> 00:05:10.553
I'm gonna throw this at
you one more time, okay?

00:05:10.560 --> 00:05:11.400
- Okay.

00:05:13.100 --> 00:05:14.940
[laughter]

00:05:15.260 --> 00:05:17.750
- The question that lab
needs to answer is:

00:05:17.750 --> 00:05:20.000
Can humans adapt to that over time?

00:05:20.000 --> 00:05:23.190
And if so, how long does it take them
to come back down to Earth?

00:05:23.190 --> 00:05:25.144
Let's bring this to a stop!

00:05:25.144 --> 00:05:27.840
Avi, can you bring us to zero?

00:05:27.840 --> 00:05:29.690
Bring your hands down and don't move.

00:05:32.901 --> 00:05:34.093
- I swear, the room is tilting--

00:05:34.093 --> 00:05:35.560
- Don't move!

00:05:35.560 --> 00:05:36.673
Oh, that's weird.

00:05:37.780 --> 00:05:41.852
- And we're stopped,
so try to swing your arms in front of you.

00:05:41.852 --> 00:05:44.845
[laughter]

00:05:44.845 --> 00:05:47.230
Oh, I don't like that.

00:05:47.230 --> 00:05:48.618
- You've been adapted.

00:05:48.618 --> 00:05:50.700
There are no unusual
forces on your arms but

00:05:50.700 --> 00:05:52.643
you feel it, right?
- But they're still, yeah,

00:05:52.643 --> 00:05:53.476
They're still doing that.

00:05:53.476 --> 00:05:54.646
- And now you have to re-adapt.

00:05:54.646 --> 00:05:57.900
- Thank you so, so much.

00:05:57.900 --> 00:05:59.850
Thank you so much to
everyone at the Ashton Graybiel

00:05:59.850 --> 00:06:02.040
Spatial Orientation Lab
at Brandeis University.

00:06:02.040 --> 00:06:04.840
Pull down on the description
for more about them and their work.

